{
  "concept": "Principal-ray tracing shows that concave mirrors can form real or virtual images depending on object distance, while convex mirrors always form smaller upright virtual images behind the mirror.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "single horizontal optical axis",
    "pale blue-gray curved mirror arc with reflective surface indicated",
    "mirror vertex V labeled on the optical axis",
    "focal point F labeled on the optical axis",
    "center of curvature C labeled on the optical axis",
    "red upright object arrow at the left with base P and tip Q",
    "label 'Object' beside the object arrow",
    "red image arrow with base P′ and tip Q′",
    "label 'Image' beside the image arrow",
    "small drawn eye icon below the optical axis on the object side",
    "concave-mirror layout with C and F in front of the mirror on the object side",
    "convex-mirror layout with F and C behind the mirror on the far side",
    "principal ray 1 in blue with number label 1: incident ray from Q parallel to the optical axis and reflected according to the mirror type",
    "principal ray 2 in red with number label 2: ray associated with the focal point and reflected parallel to the optical axis",
    "principal ray 3 in purple with number label 3: ray associated with the center of curvature and normal incidence",
    "principal ray 4 in green with number label 4: ray striking the vertex V and reflecting symmetrically about the optical axis",
    "arrowheads showing the travel direction of each principal ray",
    "solid reflected rays in front of the mirror",
    "dashed backward extensions for virtual-ray construction in the convex case",
    "dashed backward extensions for virtual-image construction when a concave object is inside the focal length",
    "small right-angle marker showing the normal-incidence relation for ray 3 where appropriate",
    "number labels 1, 2, 3, and 4 placed near their corresponding rays",
    "panel-style labels '(a)' for concave mirror and '(b)' for convex mirror when both are shown in reference mode"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "mirrorType",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Mirror type",
      "options": [
        "Concave mirror",
        "Convex mirror"
      ],
      "default": "Concave mirror",
      "teaches": "A concave mirror can make reflected rays actually converge in front of the mirror, while a convex mirror makes reflected rays diverge so the image appears only where backward extensions meet behind the mirror"
    },
    {
      "id": "principalRaySelect",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Each principal ray follows one simple rule, and isolating a single ray makes the crowded construction readable"
    },
    {
      "id": "objectDistance",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Object distance from mirror, in focal lengths",
      "range": [
        0.5,
        5,
        0.1
      ],
      "default": 3,
      "teaches": "Moving the object relative to the focal point changes where the image forms, whether it is upright or inverted, and whether it is real or virtual"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Concave mirror: object beyond C",
      "narration": "With the object farther than two focal lengths from a concave mirror, the reflected rays really cross in front of the mirror. The image is real, inverted, and smaller, forming between C and F.",
      "state": {
        "mirrorType": "Concave mirror",
        "objectDistance": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Inspect the four principal-ray rules",
      "narration": "Click rays 1–4 one at a time to see that the construction is built from four simple rules: parallel-to-focus, focus-to-parallel, center-of-curvature return, and symmetric reflection at the vertex.",
      "state": {
        "mirrorType": "Concave mirror",
        "objectDistance": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Concave mirror: object inside F",
      "narration": "Move the object inside the focal length. The reflected rays no longer meet in front of the mirror; their backward extensions meet behind it, producing an upright virtual image.",
      "state": {
        "mirrorType": "Concave mirror",
        "objectDistance": 0.7
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Convex mirror: always virtual",
      "narration": "Switch to the convex mirror. The reflected rays spread apart for every object distance, and only their dashed backward extensions meet behind the mirror, giving a smaller upright virtual image.",
      "state": {
        "mirrorType": "Convex mirror",
        "objectDistance": 3
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A real image forms where reflected rays actually meet; a virtual image forms where only their backward extensions appear to meet.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Use one clean interactive diagram rather than two fixed stacked panels: the mirror-type toggle should switch between concave and convex geometries. Keep the object on the left and the mirror on the right, with the optical axis horizontal. Use OpenStax-style colors: blue for ray 1, red for ray 2, purple for ray 3, and green for ray 4; dim unselected rays when one ray is clicked. For a concave mirror, place F halfway between V and C on the object side; when object distance is greater than one focal length, draw the image inverted on the object side where the reflected rays intersect. When object distance is less than one focal length, draw reflected rays diverging and dashed extensions behind the mirror meeting at an upright virtual image. For a convex mirror, place F and C behind the mirror; draw solid reflected rays diverging on the object side and dashed extensions meeting behind the mirror between V and F. The red object and image arrows should resize and flip according to the mirror equation and magnification, with labels P, Q, P′, and Q′ preserved."
}

